---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const ohmCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'V', label: 'voltage V', min: 1, max: 24, step: 1, value: 12, unit: 'V' },
    { name: 'R', label: 'resistance R', min: 1, max: 100, step: 1, value: 12, unit: 'Ω' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'current I = V/R', expr: 'V/R', unit: 'A' },
    { label: 'power P = V²/R', expr: 'V*V/R', unit: 'W' },
    { label: 'power via I²R', expr: '(V/R)*(V/R)*R', unit: 'W' },
  ],
};

const ohmFlipOrder = {
  "prompt": "From flick to lap complete — put the story of a flipped switch in order:",
  "items": [
    "You flip the switch",
    "The electric field propagates around the loop at nearly light speed",
    "Every conduction electron in the loop starts drifting at once",
    "The lamp responds within milliseconds",
    "One individual electron finally completes a full lap — almost an hour later"
  ],
  "why": "The field travels, not the electrons. Their drift speed is under a millimetre per second, so the whole loop starts moving together in milliseconds while a single electron needs the best part of an hour for one lap of a torch circuit."
};

const voltCloze = {
  "text": "A volt is a joule per %%0%%: a 12 V battery transfers %%1%% J of energy to every coulomb that passes through it, and under heavy load its terminal voltage %%2%% because of its internal resistance.",
  "answers": [
    [
      "coulomb"
    ],
    [
      "12"
    ],
    [
      "sags",
      "drops",
      "falls"
    ]
  ],
  "hint": "Think energy-per-charge, the battery's rating, and what a starter motor does to terminal voltage."
};

const heatSpot = {
  "prompt": "This heater answer contains one faulty line — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "A heater draws I = 4 A on a 230 V supply, so R = V/I = 57.5 Ω.",
    "Its power is P = V·I = 230 × 4 = 920 W.",
    "A repair shortens the element, halving R; the current doubles to 8 A.",
    "The power doubles too, to 1,840 W — at fixed voltage, power scales linearly with resistance.",
    "That is exactly why a short circuit, with R collapsing toward zero, is dangerous."
  ],
  "errorLine": 3,
  "correction": "At fixed voltage the power QUADRUPLES, to 3,680 W — P = V²/R, so halving R multiplies power by four.",
  "why": "P = V²/R: power scales inversely with resistance at fixed voltage. Halve R → double I → the power jumps fourfold — the lesson's 12 Ω → 4 Ω example did exactly this (12 W → 36 W would be thirds; halves give fours)."
};

const flowJudge = {
  "statements": [
    {
      "s": "Current gets used up as it travels around a series circuit.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "Charge is conserved — the current is the same everywhere in the loop. What each component takes its share of is energy."
    },
    {
      "s": "Voltage is a property of two points, not a substance that flows.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "Current flows; voltage drives. 'Voltage flows' is the tell-tale sign of the misconception."
    },
    {
      "s": "A battery moves charge that is already packed into the wires; what depletes is its chemistry.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "The battery is an energy pump with a finite fuel supply — it exhausts reactions, not charge."
    },
    {
      "s": "A car battery holds its terminal voltage constant no matter how much current is drawn.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "Internal resistance makes terminal voltage sag under heavy load — cranking a starter can pull 12.6 V down to near 10 V."
    }
  ]
};

const kettleEstimate = {
  "prompt": "A 2 kW heater runs for 3 hours. Roughly how many joules of energy did it burn?",
  "unit": "MJ",
  "answer": 21.6,
  "factor": 2,
  "why": "3 hours × 2 kW = 6 kWh, and 1 kWh = 3.6 MJ, so 6 × 3.6 = 21.6 MJ.",
  "context": "That is why bills are written in kilowatt-hours — nobody wants to pay for 21,600,000 joules in words."
};

const fusePredict = {
  "q": "A 3 A appliance keeps blowing its fuse, so a friend fits a 13 A fuse 'so it stops blowing'. What did that actually change?",
  "choices": [
    "The circuit is now protected with a safer margin",
    "The cable can overheat and burn before the fuse ever melts",
    "The appliance now draws less current than before"
  ],
  "answer": 1,
  "why": "A fuse guards the cable, not your patience. Four times the design current can now cook the cable while the 13 A wire sits there unmelting — the correct move is matching the fuse to the thinnest link it guards."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/intermediate/circuits-and-ohms-law">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate physics lesson. It assumes the foundational sequence,
    especially <em>Energy and Its Conservation</em> — voltage will be defined as energy
    per unit charge, and power as energy per second, exactly as that lesson set them up
    — plus
    everyday arithmetic (multiply, divide, one square root). No prior contact with
    circuits is assumed; charge, field and current are all built here.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define voltage, current and resistance, and calculate with V = IR;</li>
    <li>explain what a battery really sells and why current is the same everywhere in a series loop;</li>
    <li>compute electrical power three ways and convert between kilowatt-hours and joules;</li>
    <li>analyse series and parallel combinations using Kirchhoff's two conservation rules;</li>
    <li>read household wiring — fuses, earth, RCD — as physics wearing regulation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    An electric circuit is a closed loop in which charge flows. <strong>Voltage</strong> is the
    push (energy per unit charge, in volts), <strong>current</strong> is the flow (charge per
    second, in amperes), and <strong>resistance</strong> is the opposition (volts needed per
    ampere, in ohms) — and Ohm's law ties them: V = IR. Electrical power, the rate energy is
    delivered, comes out as P = VI = I²R — the reason wires warm, toasters toast, and bills
    arrive. The whole subject is bookkeeping with two conserved quantities — charge and
    energy — and this chapter builds the ledgers: what current really is, what a battery
    really sells, how components combine, and how the wiring keeps you alive.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Ohm's law as a workbench — drag voltage and resistance, watch current and heat respond" widget="sliderLab" config={ohmCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> fix the resistance and slide the voltage up in even
    steps — the current readout climbs in a straight line, and that linearity
    <em>is</em> Ohm's law. Then fix the voltage and slide the resistance: current falls
    in exact inverse proportion. Try to make the two power readouts disagree; you
    cannot, and the reason why is the paragraph below.
  </p>

  <p>
    Both power readouts agree (P = VI = I²R = V²/R) — pick whichever suits the variables you
    control. Notice the asymmetry: for fixed voltage, halving the resistance doubles current
    but <em>quadruples</em> power. That is why a short circuit (R → 0) is dangerous.
  </p>

  <h2>Current: what is actually flowing</h2>
  <p>
    Current is charge in transit: <strong>I = Q/t</strong>, one ampere being one coulomb of
    charge (about 6.2 × 10¹⁸ electrons) per second. In a metal the movers are conduction
    electrons — but here is the surprise worth owning: their <strong>drift speed is under a
    millimetre per second</strong>. Flip a light switch and the light responds in
    milliseconds because the <em>field</em> that sets electrons moving propagates through the
    circuit at nearly light speed — every electron in the loop starts drifting at once, like
    water already filling a pipe when you open the valve. The electrons themselves take
    almost an hour to complete a lap of a torch circuit. Two more definitions while we're
    precise: <strong>conventional current</strong> runs from + to − (the historical
    convention, opposite to the electron flow), and direct current (DC, one direction —
    batteries) differs from alternating current (AC, cycling 50 or 60 times per second —
    the grid), a distinction that matters the moment transformers appear in the magnetism
    chapter.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="rebuild the switch-flip sequence — field first, electrons later" widget="order" config={ohmFlipOrder} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> current is charge in transit — the movers drift slowly, but
    the field that starts them travels near light speed, and the same current runs
    through every point of a series loop. The next question is what keeps them moving:
    energy, paid per coulomb.
  </p>

  <h2>Voltage: the currency of energy</h2>
  <p>
    A volt is a joule per coulomb: <strong>V = E/Q</strong>. A 12 V battery transfers 12 J of
    energy to every coulomb that passes through it; run 5 C through and 60 J have been
    delivered — the entire meaning of "voltage". Strictly, the battery's
    <strong>electromotive force</strong> (emf) is the energy given to each coulomb
    <em>by</em> the source, while a <strong>potential difference</strong> across a component
    is the energy taken <em>from</em> each coulomb by that component: around a complete loop
    the two balance, which is Kirchhoff's voltage rule in one sentence. Household mains is
    230 V in most of the world (120 V in the US — lower voltage means higher current for the
    same power, which shapes every wiring standard). And the anatomy of a battery worth
    knowing: it has a small <strong>internal resistance</strong>, so the voltage you measure
    at its terminals sags under heavy load — a car battery asked to crank a starter (hundreds
    of amps) drops from 12.6 V to near 10 V for the duration. Batteries do not "run out of
    current"; they exhaust the chemical reactions that supply energy.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="fill the blanks — what a volt actually is" widget="cloze" config={voltCloze} />

  <h2>Resistance and Ohm's law</h2>
  <p>
    Resistance is defined by the ratio <strong>R = V/I</strong>, and Georg Ohm's 1827
    discovery was that for metals at constant temperature this ratio is <em>constant</em> —
    double the voltage, double the current, a straight-line I–V graph. Components that obey
    are <strong>ohmic</strong>; many are not. A filament bulb's resistance rises as it heats
    (its I–V curve flattens at high voltage: hot lattice, more scattering, about 15× from
    cold to glowing); a diode passes current essentially one way only; a thermistor's
    resistance falls as it warms (the cheap electronic thermometer). Resistance itself
    depends on the wire: longer wires resist more (proportionally), fatter wires resist
    less (inversely to cross-sectional area), and material matters enormously — nichrome
    (toaster wire) is about 60 times more resistive than copper, which is why one glows and
    the other doesn't. Worked example from the widget: 12 V across 12 Ω gives I = 1 A and
    P = 12 W; drop R to 4 Ω and the current triples to 3 A while the power jumps ninefold
    to 36 W — the quadruple-the-heat warning of the short circuit, visible on sliders.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="find the faulty line — heater resistance and power" widget="spot-error" config={heatSpot} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "current gets used up around the circuit." Nothing of the
  sort: charge is conserved, the current in a series loop is the <em>same everywhere</em>,
  and what gets "used up" is energy — each component takes its share of each coulomb's
  energy budget. Equally wrong: "voltage flows". Voltage is a property of two points, not a
  substance; current flows, voltage <em>drives</em>.</p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a battery runs out of charge, like a tank emptying."
  Charge is conserved and is already packed into the wires; a battery moves it, it does
  not spend it. What depletes is the chemical reactions that lift each coulomb's energy
  — the battery is an energy pump with a finite fuel supply, not a reservoir of electric
  fluid that drains away.</p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="true or false — what flows, what runs out" widget="judge" config={flowJudge} />

  <h2>Power, energy and the electricity bill</h2>
  <p>
    Power is energy per second: <strong>P = VI = I²R = V²/R</strong> — three forms of one
    identity, chosen for the numbers at hand. A 3 kW kettle on 230 V mains draws I = 3000/230
    ≈ 13 A, which is why it needs its own thick cable and high-rated fuse: house ring mains
    are rated at 13 A per socket (UK) for exactly this arithmetic. Energy sold is measured in
    <strong>kilowatt-hours</strong> (1 kWh = 3.6 MJ): a 2 kW heater for 3 hours is 6 kWh,
    about £1.50 at typical tariffs. Running the numbers is a superpower: a 100 W bulb left
    on all year burns 876 kWh — more than a fridge — which is why LED retrofits (same light,
    10 W) changed national electricity demand.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="call the magnitude — the kettle's energy appetite" widget="estimate" config={kettleEstimate} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> one component at a time — push, flow, opposition, and the
    price of energy. Real circuits combine components, and there are only two ways to do
    it: one path or several.
  </p>

  <h2>Series and parallel: the two ways to combine</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Series (in a row):</strong> one path; resistances add (R = R₁ + R₂); the
    current is the same everywhere; voltages divide across components in proportion to their
    resistances. Worked example: 6 Ω and 3 Ω in series across 12 V: total 9 Ω, so I = 1.33 A;
    the 6 Ω takes 8 V, the 3 Ω takes 4 V. Old-style Christmas lights were wired like this:
    one dead bulb breaks the only path, and everything goes dark.</li>
    <li><strong>Parallel (side by side):</strong> multiple paths; 1/R_total = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂
    (the total is always <em>smaller</em> than the smallest branch); each branch gets the
    full supply voltage; the currents divide. The same 6 Ω and 3 Ω in parallel: 1/R = 1/6 +
    1/3 = ½, so R = 2 Ω and the pair draws 6 A from 12 V (2 A through the 6 Ω, 4 A through
    the 3 Ω — the current splits in inverse proportion to resistance). Your home is wired
    this way: every appliance gets full mains, and switching one off does not dim the rest.</li>
    <li><strong>Kirchhoff's rules</strong> solve anything more complicated: at any junction,
    current in = current out (charge conservation); around any loop, the sum of emfs equals
    the sum of drops (energy conservation). Every circuit analysis is these two ledgers
    applied relentlessly.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Circuit components and electrical safety</h2>
  <p>
    The standard symbols (cell, battery, lamp, switch, resistor, ammeter in series,
    voltmeter in parallel) exist because a circuit diagram is a legal document between
    builder and reader: ammeters must carry the whole current (tiny resistance), voltmeters
    must snoop without disturbing (huge resistance). The <strong>variable resistor</strong>
    (dimmers, volume knobs) and <strong>potential divider</strong> (two resistors tapping a
    fraction of the supply voltage — every sensor reading ever taken) round out the
    toolkit.
  </p>
  <p>
    Safety is this chapter's applied half, and every rule is an equation wearing a
    regulation. Mains has a <strong>live</strong> wire at 230 V relative to earth and a
    <strong>neutral</strong> near 0 V; the <strong>earth wire</strong> bonds metal casings
    to the ground so that a fault shorts live-to-earth — a huge current that blows the
    <strong>fuse</strong> instantly rather than electrocuting the next user. Fuses and
    circuit breakers are deliberate weak links rated slightly above normal load: a 13 A fuse
    in a 3 A appliance is not "safer", it is a fuse that will let the cable burn first.
    Modern installations add the <strong>RCD</strong> (residual current device), comparing
    live and neutral currents and tripping within ~30 ms if they differ — detecting current
    leaking through a person (30 mA across the heart can kill; it takes roughly 5 minutes
    of the same through a fuse wire to notice). Double-insulated appliances skip the earth
    by encasing everything in plastic. Water hazards, daisy-chained extension leads and
    "just for a second" multimeter mistakes across a mains socket are all the same failure:
    a low-resistance path where the equations promised high.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="bet first — what does the bigger fuse actually protect?" widget="predict" config={fusePredict} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a bigger fuse is safer." A fuse is a deliberate weak
  link rated slightly above the circuit's normal current, and it protects the cable, not
  the appliance's comfort: a 13 A fuse in a 3 A appliance will happily let the cable cook
  before it melts. The correct move is matching the fuse to the thinnest link it guards.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A 12 V supply drives a current of 2 A through a resistor. What is the resistance, and what power does it dissipate?</li>
    <li>A kettle draws about 13 A on 230 V mains. What power is that, and why does it need its own thick cable?</li>
    <li>A 6 Ω and a 3 Ω resistor sit in series across 12 V. Why is the current identical through both, and what actually gets used up?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) R = V/I = 12/2 = 6 Ω; P = VI = 24 W — check with I²R
    = 4 × 6 = 24 W: the three forms agree. (2) P = VI ≈ 3,000 W; that current runs
    through the cable, and any resistance in it dissipates I²R as heat, so a thin or
    undersized cable warms dangerously — thickness ratings are I²R arithmetic. (3) Charge
    is conserved and has only one path, so the same coulombs per second pass through both
    resistors; what gets used up is energy — each coulomb delivers its 12 J budget across
    the pair, 8 V worth in the 6 Ω and 4 V worth in the 3 Ω.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Ohm's law V = IR: for metals at constant temperature the ratio is constant — an ohmic component has a straight-line I–V graph.</li>
    <li>Current is charge per second; conduction electrons drift under a millimetre per second while the field propagates near light speed.</li>
    <li>Voltage is energy per coulomb: the emf gives each coulomb energy, and components take it back around the loop.</li>
    <li>P = VI = I²R = V²/R — three forms of one identity; halving resistance at fixed voltage quadruples power.</li>
    <li>Series: one path, resistances add, current common. Parallel: many paths, the total is smaller than the smallest branch, each branch sees the full voltage.</li>
    <li>Kirchhoff's rules are bookkeeping for conserved charge and energy — they solve any circuit.</li>
    <li>Safety devices are equations in disguise: fuses matched to the cable, earth wires that short faults, RCDs comparing live and neutral.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The field that pushed these electrons is the next subject: <em>Magnetism &amp;
    Induction</em> picks up the moving charge and shows the forces, motors and
    transformers it makes — including the transformer that lets the grid defeat the I²R
    tax computed here. For charge at rest before it flows, the sibling lesson
    <em>Electrostatics</em> is the bridge back.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Why the grid transmits at colossal voltage.</strong> Power lost in a cable is
    I²R — the current's square. To deliver 1 GW through a cable of 0.1 Ω at 230 V would
    need I = 4.3 MA and dissipate more power than the planet's electricity use. Step the
    voltage up to 400,000 V instead and the same power needs 2.5 kA, losing 0.6 MW — 0.06%.
    The transformer (magnetism chapter) makes the voltage swap cheap, and it is the reason
    pylons exist: high voltage is not for power line aesthetics but for the I²R tax. This
    is also the historical reason AC won the "war of the currents" against Edison's DC —
    transformers only work with changing currents.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Two bulbs, one counterintuitive comparison.</strong> Old filament bulbs rated
    100 W and 60 W at the same 230 V: which has the higher resistance? From P = V²/R, the
    <em>lower</em> power bulb has the higher resistance (881 Ω vs 529 Ω). Brightness and
    resistance run oppositely at fixed voltage — a two-line exam question that trips half of
    every class, and a good test of whether you manipulate equations or understand them.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Superconductors: resistance zero.</strong> Below a critical temperature (lead:
    7 K; niobium alloys: 9–20 K; cuprate ceramics: up to about 93 K, achievable with liquid
    nitrogen), some materials conduct with <em>exactly</em> zero resistance — current flows
    in a loop for years without decay (rings installed in the 1980s still carry it). MRI
    scanners, maglev prototypes and the LHC's magnets all run on it. The mechanism (electrons
    pairing into bosons, per BCS theory) is quantum; the open question is practical: a
    room-temperature, ambient-pressure superconductor remains undiscovered — every claimed
    breakthrough of the last decade has failed replication, a live demonstration of the
    research-methods chapter running in materials science.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Ohm's own rough ride.</strong> Ohm published in 1827 as a schoolteacher and was
    received so badly (one reviewer called the work "a web of naked fancies") that he
    resigned his post; the Prussian establishment took years to accept what every multimeter
    now states as a dial. The lesson travels: a linear law hiding in a messy world (contact
    resistances, unstable early cells, heating effects) took careful isolation of variables
    to see — the experimental method, not genius, was the hero. Resistance, meanwhile, kept
    its name honestly: it resists our models too.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Ohm (1827); OpenStax, College Physics (ch. 20, Current and Resistance; ch. 21, Circuits); IET Wiring Regulations summaries; National Grid transmission documentation. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
